92186139325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "23dd4876-d6d3-50c1-a5c6-9d5d709220d3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186138410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139329 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186139293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139329 #edb/ref 1061 18735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139329 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bb62a766-8bbc-56b7-a5a5-1db2c882c43d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139329 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186139645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139645 #edb/ref 1064 "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $2x^3-2x.$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $2x^3-2x$ factors into $2x(x+1)(x-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139645 #edb/ref 1065 3366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139645 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "85714b2f-9623-52ab-aa32-df1827f75877" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186139645 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529598 #edb/ref 1080 "{v}^{3}(3v+1)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529598 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529598 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0b2d79c1-583e-57ab-ba0d-3a6a96d759e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529674 #edb/ref 1080 "4{z}^{2}(x-3)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529674 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529674 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bc95d9d-5eae-5413-94e8-4ae447be363d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529692 #edb/ref 1080 "4{y}^{2}(y-3)^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186529692 #edb/r