ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530401 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6990042-c99e-5c76-82b8-8db0cdb93f3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530422 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530422 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111943" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530422 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530422 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $9{v}^{5}+6{v}^{4}+{v}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530422 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0a134bf3-f015-57aa-8456-af35dcce2abe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530422 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${v}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${v}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}^{5}+6{v}^{4}+{v}^{3} & ={v}^{3}(9{v}^{2}+6v+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $9{v}^{2}+6v+1$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}^{3}(9{v}^{2}+6v+1) & = \\\\ {v}^{3}((3v)^{2}+6v+{1}^{2}) & = \\\\ {v}^{3}((3v)^{2}+2\\cdot (3v)+{1}^{2}) & = \\\\ {v}^{3}(3v+1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530483 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530483 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111945" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530483 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530483 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $4{y}^{4}-12{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530483 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "831d09f3-e78e-5131-b5ea-583953555624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530483 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${y}^{2}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${y}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{4}-12{y}^{3}+9{y}^{2} & ={y}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-12y+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $4{y}^{2}-12y+9$ is a perfect square trinomial that can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}(4{y}^{2}-12y+9) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}((2y)^{2}-12y+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}((2y)^{2}-2\\cdot 3\\cdot (2y)+{3}^{2}) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}(2y-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530484 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530484 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530484 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530484 #edb/ref 1071 "The trinomial $4{x}^{2}{z}^{6}+8x{z}^{6}-60{z}^{6}$ is equivalent to" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530484 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "879afca9-fcee-5d1d-bc32-90b5ad8c970f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530484 #edb/ref 1074 "Note that all of the terms have a common factor of $4{z}^{6}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $4{z}^{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{z}^{6}+8x{z}^{6}-60{z}^{6}=(4{z}^{6})\\cdot {x}^{2}+(4{z}^{6})\\cdot 2x-(4{z}^{6})\\cdot 15=4{z}^{6}({x}^{2}+2x-15).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the polynomial ${x}^{2}+2x-15,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{z}^{6}({x}^{2}+2x-15) & = \\\\ 4{z}^{6}(({x}^{2}+5x)+(-3x-15)) & = \\\\ 4{z}^{6}(x(x+5)-3(x+5)) & = \\\\ 4{z}^{6}(x-3)(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530316 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-111921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530489 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $9{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8dcc5eab-0d17-5264-bead-34e7ca604fff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530489 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${n}^{2}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${n}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n9{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}={n}^{2}(9{n}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $9{n}^{2}-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}(9{n}^{2}-1)={n}^{2}((3n)^{2}-{1}^{2})={n}^{2}(3n+1)(3n-1)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-69586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530528 #edb/ref 1071 "Factor the expression $9{y}^{5}-{y}^{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d4ff2f27-cc03-59a8-bdee-fa30389e7f38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186530528 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of ${y}^{3}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the ${y}^{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n9{y}^{5}-{y}^{3}={y}^{3}(9{y}^{2}-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $9{y}^{2}-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}(9{y}^{2}-1)={y}^{3}((3y)^{2}-{1}^{2})={y}^{3}(3y+1)(3y-1)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
